# Protections for the symlink-following-write corpus (CWE-59). Held IDENTICAL
# -- byte-identical, not merely equivalent -- across every target in this
# family on purpose: the *write mechanism that follows a planted symlink* is
# the only variable, so a protection difference would confound the
# measurement. The negative control shares this exact file too, which is what
# makes its refusal attributable to the absence of the bug (O_NOFOLLOW is
# present) rather than to different hardening.
#
# EVERY PROTECTION IS ON, AND NONE OF THEM MATTERS. That is the point of the
# category, not an oversight -- exactly as in benchmark/corpus_toctou/, this
# category is orthogonal to every memory-safety mitigation there is:
#
# -fstack-protector-all  a canary detects a corrupted return address in an
#                        epilogue. Nothing in this category writes past a
#                        buffer, so no frame is ever damaged and the check
#                        always passes. The exploit is a symlink() call and a
#                        line of text.
# -pie -fPIE             PIE randomises where code and data live. No exploit
#                        step here needs any address at all: the only
#                        "payload" is a FILENAME planted ahead of time, and
#                        there is no timing to get right either -- it just has
#                        to exist before the program's open()/creat()/fopen()
#                        call runs once.
# -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now    Full RELRO write-protects the GOT after startup.
#                        There is no GOT write in this category, no pointer
#                        write of any kind; the program calls exactly the
#                        functions it was linked against. It simply resolves
#                        a NAME whose meaning an attacker changed in advance.
# (NX)                   comes from the default ELF program headers; nothing
#                        is ever executed from a data page, so it is
#                        satisfied too.
#
# Building the family with the full modern set keeps "hardened" from being
# quietly re-learned as "not exploitable" -- and here it is stronger than
# that: it shows that for this category the entire memory-safety mitigation
# stack is not merely insufficient, it is *irrelevant*, because none of it is
# anywhere near the exploit's path.
#
# -O0                    load-bearing for the MEASUREMENT, not for the bug.
#                        The executor derives the write's path build site by
#                        matching the path ARGUMENT of the open/creat/fopen
#                        call to the same frame slot the preceding snprintf()
#                        wrote into (`lea -0xNN(%rbp)`); at higher -O the
#                        buffer can live in a register or be folded away, and
#                        the derivation would have nothing to match. The bug
#                        itself -- resolving an attacker-plantable name with
#                        no O_NOFOLLOW and no prior lstat -- is entirely
#                        unaffected by optimisation level.
-fstack-protector-all
-pie
-fPIE
-Wl,-z,relro,-z,now
-O0
